From 28105b2e5e1662e78ecdbbcf16377a10c740a811 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Willie Marchetto <orange@devbuildzero.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 20:39:03 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] fix(config): make bd config unset remove nested keys, and
 report what it did

`bd config unset <dotted.key>` reported success and removed nothing whenever
the key was stored in nested form. The value stayed live, and for sync.remote
-- a Dolt PUSH TARGET -- `bd dolt remote remove origin` went on to commit the
unchanged, git-tracked .beads/config.yaml with the message
"bd: clear sync.remote", propagating the still-configured target to every
clone under a commit saying it was cleared.

Two independent defects composed into the no-op:

1. commentOutYamlKey matched only `^(\s*)<literal-key>\s*:`, which can match a
   flat `sync.remote:` line and nothing else. setYamlConfigAtPath routes the
   same dotted key through updateNestedYamlKey and writes `sync:` with an
   indented `remote:`, so set and unset disagreed about where the key lives.
   Which spelling a key wears is decided by updateNestedYamlKey's
   `len(root.Content) == 0` early return: the first dotted key written into an
   empty config.yaml lands flat and stays flat, every later one lands nested.
   So removability depended on the order keys were first written, months
   earlier -- which is why two adjacent dolt.* keys in one file behaved
   differently and the failure looked intermittent.

2. commentOutYamlKey returned only a string, so "matched nothing" was not
   representable. UnsetYamlConfig could not detect it and cmd/bd/config.go
   printed "Unset %s" unconditionally. Fixing (1) alone would still report a
   removal for a key that was never set.

commentOutYamlKey now resolves the nested path through the YAML node tree and
comments out the key line plus its whole value block, and returns whether it
matched. The nested arm walks the tree rather than widening the regex to the
leaf name: `^(\s*)remote\s*:` would also match `remote:` under any other
parent, so unsetting sync.remote could clear an unrelated key. Both spellings
are commented when both are present -- viper resolves a duplicated key to the
flat one, so removing only the nested copy would leave the value live.

`bd config unset` now prints "<key> was not set" instead of "Unset <key>" when
nothing matched, and reports `removed` in --json. Exit status stays 0 either
way so unset remains idempotent for provisioning scripts; the change is to
stop claiming a removal that did not happen. Side-effect advice is printed
only on an actual removal, since it describes what a removal implies.
`bd dolt remote remove origin` commits config.yaml only when the clear
changed it, and otherwise warns that the push target is still configured.

Two adjacent defects fall out of the same rewrite. A key that matches nothing
now leaves the file untouched instead of rewriting it byte-identically, and
the terminal newline is no longer stripped from a git-tracked file on every
unset. commentOutYamlKey no longer uses a bufio.Scanner, whose default 64 KiB
token cap would have silently discarded everything past an over-long line and
written the truncated file anyway, with no scanner.Err() check to notice.
updateYamlKey still uses a Scanner and is left alone.

Verified: TestUnsetYamlConfigRemovesWhatSetYamlConfigWrote drives the real
SetYamlConfig/UnsetYamlConfig pair and asserts the key is unbound afterward.
It fails on the parent commit for all three of sync.remote, dolt.local-only
and backup.enabled, and passes here. The pre-existing TestUnsetYamlConfig did
not catch this because its fixture hand-writes the flat spelling that
SetYamlConfig would never have produced. Also confirmed end to end by
building bd from this tree and re-running the original report: the nested key
is removed, `bd config get` reports not-set, the trailing newline survives,
unsetting a never-set key reports "was not set" at exit 0, and the flat path
is unchanged. `go test ./internal/config/` and `go vet` are clean.
